<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Sashimi Simulation Design &mdash; Conceptual Model</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #faf9f7;
    --surface: #ffffff;
    --ink: #1f2328;
    --ink-soft: #57606a;
    --ink-faint: #8b949e;
    --rule: #d8dde3;
    --rule-soft: #e8ecef;
    --accent: #0b6e6e;
    --accent-soft: #e2f0ef;
    --flag: #9a3412;
    --flag-bg: #fdf3ec;
    --flag-rule: #e8cdb8;
    --port-bg: #f2f4f6;
    --port-ink: #45505c;
    --ecb-ink: #7d8590;
    --chain-bg: #f5f3ee;
    --badge-init: #5b5bd6; --badge-reset: #a26a00; --badge-move: #0b6e6e;
    --badge-act: #b4304a; --badge-finalize: #6f42c1; --badge-client: #6e7781;
    --mono: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    --sans: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root {
      --bg: #14171b;
      --surface: #1b2026;
      --ink: #e6e9ec;
      --ink-soft: #a8b1ba;
      --ink-faint: #6e7781;
      --rule: #333b44;
      --rule-soft: #262d34;
      --accent: #4fc3b8;
      --accent-soft: #16332f;
      --flag: #f0a175;
      --flag-bg: #2a2019;
      --flag-rule: #4a382a;
      --port-bg: #21272e;
      --port-ink: #9aa6b2;
      --ecb-ink: #6e7781;
      --chain-bg: #22262b;
      --badge-init: #9d9df2; --badge-reset: #d6a545; --badge-move: #4fc3b8;
      --badge-act: #ee7f96; --badge-finalize: #b392f0; --badge-client: #8b949e;
    }
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }
  main { max-width: 78ch; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 1.5rem 5rem; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.75rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 3rem 0 1rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  h2 .secno { color: var(--ink-faint); font-weight: 500; margin-right: 0.5em; }
  p { margin: 0.75rem 0; }
  code, .sys { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.86em; }
  code { background: var(--port-bg); padding: 0.1em 0.35em; border-radius: 4px; }
  a { color: var(--accent); }

  .kicker {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .subtitle { color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0.25rem 0 0; }
  .meta { color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.85rem; margin-top: 1rem; font-family: var(--mono); }
  .review-note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem; padding: 0.85rem 1.1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft);
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  /* ---- pipeline ---- */
  .pipeline { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .tick-cap {
    text-align: center; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint);
    padding: 0.4rem 0;
  }
  .group {
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .group-head {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.75rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .group-no {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--ink-faint);
    flex: none;
  }
  .group-name { font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .group-tag { color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.8rem; }
  .badge {
    margin-left: auto; flex: none;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 99px;
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
  }
  .b-init { color: var(--badge-init); }
  .b-reset { color: var(--badge-reset); }
  .b-move { color: var(--badge-move); }
  .b-act { color: var(--badge-act); }
  .b-finalize { color: var(--badge-finalize); }
  .b-client { color: var(--badge-client); }
  .purpose { margin: 0.25rem 0 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .purpose strong { color: var(--ink); }

  .members { margin: 0 0 1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem; }
  .member { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(15ch, 20ch) 1fr; gap: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .member .sys { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
  .member .role { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .member { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.1rem; }
  }
  .chain {
    background: var(--chain-bg); border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.9;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .chain b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

  .port {
    margin: 0 -1.25rem; padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem 0.7rem;
    background: var(--port-bg); border-top: 1px dashed var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--port-ink); line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .port .plabel {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-right: 0.5em;
  }
  .port .slot { font-family: var(--mono); white-space: nowrap; }
  .port .slot i { font-style: normal; color: var(--ink-faint); }

  .ecb {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 0.35rem 0.5rem; color: var(--ecb-ink);
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .ecb::before, .ecb::after { content: ""; flex: 1; border-top: 1px dashed var(--rule); }
  .flow {
    text-align: center; color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1; padding: 0.3rem 0;
  }

  .predicted {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.75rem; margin: 0.35rem 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--surface) 40%, var(--bg));
  }
  .predicted > .pred-label {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    padding: 0.1rem 0.25rem 0.6rem;
  }
  .predicted > .pred-label span { font-weight: 400; text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0; color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .predicted .group { margin: 0; }

  /* ---- principles ---- */
  .principles { padding-left: 0; margin: 1rem 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: p; }
  .principles > li {
    counter-increment: p; position: relative;
    padding: 0 0 0 2.4rem; margin: 1.1rem 0;
  }
  .principles > li::before {
    content: counter(p);
    position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0.1rem;
    width: 1.6rem; height: 1.6rem; border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 700;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  }
  .principles b { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.15rem; }
  .principles p { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; }

  /* ---- flags ---- */
  .flags { display: grid; gap: 0.75rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .flag-item {
    border: 1px solid var(--flag-rule); border-left: 3px solid var(--flag);
    background: var(--flag-bg); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem; font-size: 0.93rem;
  }
  .flag-item b { color: var(--flag); font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.78rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; display: block; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }

  footer {
    margin-top: 4rem; padding-top: 1.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.82rem; line-height: 1.7;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<header>
  <div class="kicker">Sashimi &middot; Unity ECS &rarr; flecs-ecs port</div>
  <h1>Sashimi Simulation Design &mdash; Conceptual Model</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">System groups, responsibilities, ordering, and the one-tick dataflow &mdash;
  Claude's interpretation of the Unity design, mapped to the flecs-ecs port.</p>
  <div class="meta">2026-07-05</div>
  <div class="review-note">
    Written for the author's review: this page states how I <em>read</em> the design.
    If a section misstates intent, that is exactly the feedback this page exists to collect.
  </div>
</header>

<h2 id="tick-model"><span class="secno">1</span>The tick model</h2>

<p>The simulation is an authoritative <strong>60&nbsp;Hz tick</strong>. Content times are
authored in seconds and converted by the tick rate (<code>ticks = seconds &times; 60</code>),
so design values stay human-readable and the tick rate stays a single constant.</p>

<p>A second clock rides on top: the <strong>4&nbsp;Hz periodic tick</strong> (0.25&nbsp;s),
which drives aura and poison damage. This decouples damage-over-time cadence from frame
rate &mdash; DoT frequency is a design constant, not a frame artifact. Both clocks first
fire at tick&nbsp;15.</p>

<p><strong>Prediction/rollback is deprecated.</strong> Networked play has been abandoned,
so netcode prediction is no longer needed and is being retired. The predicted chain
(groups 2&ndash;7 below) and its once-per-authoritative-tick gates are artifacts of the
networked era. The port's model &mdash; one world where every tick is authoritative,
one engine turn == one tick, every gate trivially true &mdash; is the intended
end-state of the design, not a simplification of it.</p>

<h2 id="pipeline"><span class="secno">2</span>The pipeline &mdash; one tick, top to bottom</h2>

<p>Groups in execution order. Each card names the Unity group, its purpose, and its
member systems; the shaded strip at the bottom of each card is where that group's work
lands in the flecs-ecs port (engine phase + <code>system_order</code> slots). Between
every predicted group there is an <strong>ECB (entity command buffer) playback
boundary</strong>: structural changes &mdash; entity create/destroy &mdash; are deferred
to the group end, and new entities become visible to the next group at the earliest,
usually next tick.</p>

<div class="pipeline">
  <div class="tick-cap">&#9660; tick N begins &#9660;</div>

  <!-- 1. BeginInitialization -->
  <section class="group">
    <div class="group-head">
      <span class="group-no">1</span>
      <span class="group-name">BeginInitializationSimulationSystemGroup</span>
      <span class="group-tag">frame start, InitializationSystemGroup &middot;
        conceptually: MetaInitializationSimulationGroup</span>
      <span class="badge b-init">Port: Init</span>
    </div>
    <p class="purpose">Handle the events and functions that <strong>surround</strong>
    the turn-based simulation itself &mdash; gathering input data, and handling
    transitions such as starting and ending the game. This group interfaces the game
    with everything around it: possession, pause, game over.</p>
    <div class="members">
      <div class="member"><span class="sys">ClientGameInputSystem</span>
        <span class="role">input capture; octant snap to 45 degrees</span></div>
      <div class="member"><span class="sys">ServerPossessSystem / ServerPauseSystem</span>
        <span class="role">RPC-driven flow control</span></div>
      <div class="member"><span class="sys">ServerGameOverSystem</span>
        <span class="role">victory/defeat check; reads last tick's time</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-init">Init phase</span>
      <span class="slot">1 <i>rewind</i></span> (engine) &middot;
      <span class="slot">2 <i>app_start</i></span> (once) &middot;
      <span class="slot">3 <i>game_time</i></span> &middot;
      <span class="slot">4 <i>hero_ai</i></span> &mdash; headless input stand-in;
      obeys the observability contract: on-field reads only, no future knowledge.
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="flow">&#9660;</div>

  <!-- Predicted wrapper -->
  <div class="predicted">
    <div class="pred-label">PredictedSimulationSystemGroup
      <span>&mdash; rollback-era wrapper, deprecated with networked play; contains
      groups 2&ndash;7. Its systems stay deterministic per tick, but the port's
      one-world / every-tick-authoritative model is the intended end-state, not a
      simplification.</span></div>

    <!-- 2. FirstSimulation -->
    <section class="group">
      <div class="group-head">
        <span class="group-no">2</span>
        <span class="group-name">FirstSimulationSystemGroup</span>
        <span class="group-tag">conceptually: TurnInitializationSimulationGroup
          (author: name could be better; leaving as is)</span>
        <span class="badge b-init">Port: Init</span>
      </div>
      <p class="purpose"><strong>Between-turns work</strong> and initialization of
      entities: establish this tick's time and finish initializing anything born last
      tick. Gated to run exactly once per simulation tick.</p>
      <div class="members">
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">GameTimeSystem_FixedStep</span>
          <span class="role">tick++; sets the 4&nbsp;Hz periodic-clock flag</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">UnitInitializationSystem</span>
          <span class="role">bake level/stat multipliers at spawn</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">InitializeCollisionSystem</span>
          <span class="role">collision setup for newly born entities</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-init">Init phase</span>
        also lands in Init: <span class="slot">3 <i>game_time</i></span>.
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ecb">ECB playback &mdash; deferred creates/destroys commit</div>

    <!-- 3. WorldInitialization -->
    <section class="group">
      <div class="group-head">
        <span class="group-no">3</span>
        <span class="group-name">WorldInitializationSystemGroup</span>
        <span class="badge b-reset">Port: Reset</span>
      </div>
      <p class="purpose"><strong>Beginning-of-turn concepts</strong> necessary so
      simulation of the turn can occur &mdash; arm intents and effects before the world
      moves. Ready-checks here deliberately read <strong>last tick's spatial
      snapshot</strong> (one-tick staleness, in parity with the port's Reset phase).</p>
      <div class="members">
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">AbilitySystem</span>
          <span class="role">stage machine: windup / active / recovery</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">ProjectileSpawnRequestSystem</span>
          <span class="role">weapon fire; starts on cooldown</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">DestroyGhostOnTimer / AtTick</span>
          <span class="role">timed despawn marking</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-reset">Reset phase</span>
        <span class="slot">5 <i>start_of_tick</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">6 <i>destroy_on_timer</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">7 <i>ability_machine</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">8 <i>weapon_fire</i></span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ecb">ECB playback &mdash; deferred creates/destroys commit</div>

    <!-- 4. WorldSimulation -->
    <section class="group">
      <div class="group-head">
        <span class="group-no">4</span>
        <span class="group-name">WorldSimulationSystemGroup</span>
        <span class="badge b-move">Port: Move</span>
      </div>
      <p class="purpose">Begin the turn's simulation with <strong>space, intent, and
      movement</strong> &mdash; perception &rarr; decision &rarr; motion, strictly
      ordered &mdash; before determining the gameplay consequences. The spatial hash
      is rebuilt <strong>before</strong> movement, so this tick's consumers see
      pre-move positions.</p>
      <div class="chain">pinned chain:
        <b>SpatialHashing</b> &rarr; <b>EnemyTargeting</b> &rarr;
        <b>CreatureMoveDirection</b> &rarr; <b>Move</b> &rarr;
        <b>GameplayChildOffsetSync</b></div>
      <div class="members">
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">SpatialHashingSystem</span>
          <span class="role">rebuild proximity index; cell 0.5</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">EnemyTargetingSystem</span>
          <span class="role">acquire r=5; switch hysteresis 0.15</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">CreatureMoveDirectionSystem</span>
          <span class="role">follow/flee + ally anti-encroachment steering</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">MoveSystem</span>
          <span class="role">integrate at 1/60; slow multiplier; arena bounds; co-op camera tether</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">GameplayChildOffsetSyncSystem</span>
          <span class="role">hitbox follows parent; x-flip only</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">CameraFollowPointSystem</span>
          <span class="role">possessed-hero centroid &mdash; gameplay-relevant: it is the wave-spawn origin</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-move">Move phase</span>
        <span class="slot">9 <i>spatial_hash</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">10 <i>targeting</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">11 <i>creature_steering</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">12 <i>pickup_magnet</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">13 <i>movement</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">14 <i>child_offset_sync</i></span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ecb">ECB playback &mdash; deferred creates/destroys commit</div>

    <!-- 5. EffectApplication -->
    <section class="group">
      <div class="group-head">
        <span class="group-no">5</span>
        <span class="group-name">EffectApplicationSimulationSystemGroup</span>
        <span class="badge b-act">Port: Act</span>
      </div>
      <p class="purpose">Determine <strong>all</strong> the gameplay consequences that
      will happen this turn &mdash; contacts, damage buffers, statuses, triggers,
      applied as events, never final state &mdash; so order-of-operations issues cannot
      arise. Pickup heal commits at this group's ECB, before damage resolution; the
      code calls that ordering a hack, not a rule (see flags).</p>
      <div class="members">
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">CollisionDetectionSystem</span>
          <span class="role">trigger overlap &rarr; pair events</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">CollisionResponseSystem</span>
          <span class="role">routes events; contains no unit pushback</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">ApplyDamageSystem</span>
          <span class="role">heroes take the MAX of same-tick hits; others the sum</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">OnHitEffectSystem</span>
          <span class="role">status application</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">PickupSystem</span>
          <span class="role">magnet + collect &mdash; post-move</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">per-ability effect systems</span>
          <span class="role">Slime/Wolf/LargeCube attack + charge</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-act">Act phase</span>
        <span class="slot">15 <i>collision_push</i></span> (port-added separation;
        Unity has no unit-unit pushback &mdash; it de-clumps via steering) &middot;
        <span class="slot">16 <i>damage_apply</i></span> (collapses apply + resolve
        same-tick) &middot;
        <span class="slot">17 <i>pickup_collect</i></span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ecb">ECB playback &mdash; pickup heal commits here, before resolution</div>

    <!-- 6. EffectResolution -->
    <section class="group">
      <div class="group-head">
        <span class="group-no">6</span>
        <span class="group-name">EffectResolutionSimulationSystemGroup</span>
        <span class="badge b-act">Port: Act</span>
      </div>
      <p class="purpose">Resolve <strong>all effects</strong>, so order-of-operations
      issues do not affect outcomes: convert the aggregated events into state exactly
      once &mdash; HP, death, immunity, status ticks. ResolveDamageSystem runs first;
      everything else is UpdateAfter it.</p>
      <div class="members">
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">ResolveDamageSystem</span>
          <span class="role">HP -= DamageThisTick; starts i-frames</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">DefeatSystem</span>
          <span class="role">HP &le; 0 &rarr; defeated; queues respawn</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">IFramesSystem</span>
          <span class="role">12-tick immunity countdown</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">StatusEffectSystem</span>
          <span class="role">poison on the periodic clock; expiry</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">OutOfGameSystem</span>
          <span class="role">off-field: untargetable, inert</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-act">Act phase</span>
        <span class="slot">18 <i>status_effects</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">19 <i>defeat</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">20 <i>iframes</i></span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ecb">ECB playback &mdash; deferred creates/destroys commit</div>

    <!-- 7. FullyPredicted -->
    <section class="group">
      <div class="group-head">
        <span class="group-no">7</span>
        <span class="group-name">FullyPredictedSimulationSystemGroup</span>
        <span class="group-tag">conceptually: TurnEndSimulationSystemsGroup</span>
        <span class="badge b-finalize">Port: Finalize</span>
      </div>
      <p class="purpose"><strong>Turn end</strong> &mdash; the irreversible consequences
      of the turn: births, deaths, drops, destruction. The group itself is an artifact
      of the rollback era: its goal was to avoid paying the cost of re-simulating these
      events during rollback and re-simulation (members self-gated on
      first-time-fully-predicting, not partial prediction). With rollback deprecated it
      could likely merge into EffectResolution, though there may be benefits to keeping
      a distinct turn-end group regardless.</p>
      <div class="members">
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">EnemySpawnSystem</span>
          <span class="role">wave script; spawn origin = hero centroid captured at wave advance</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">DelayedSpawnSystem</span>
          <span class="role">warning &rarr; creature 1.25&nbsp;s later</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">OnDefeatedSystem</span>
          <span class="role">pity-pool drops; kill consequences</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">RespawnSystem</span>
          <span class="role">half heal + 2&nbsp;s out-of-game</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">ProjectileLifecycleSystem</span>
          <span class="role">bounce/pierce/range expiry</span></div>
        <div class="member"><span class="sys">DestroyGhostSystem</span>
          <span class="role">deferred destruction; OrderLast</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-finalize">Finalize phase</span>
        <span class="slot">21 <i>projectile_lifecycle</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">22 <i>on_defeated</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">23 <i>respawn</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">24 <i>spawn_director</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">25 <i>delayed_spawn</i></span> &middot;
        <span class="slot">27 <i>app_stop</i></span> (end-of-tick game-over check ==
        Unity's start-of-next-tick check) &middot;
        <span class="slot">28 <i>history_capture</i></span> (engine)
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ecb">ECB playback &mdash; end of the predicted chain</div>
  </div>

  <div class="flow">&#9660;</div>

  <!-- 8. FixedStep -->
  <section class="group">
    <div class="group-head">
      <span class="group-no">8</span>
      <span class="group-name">FixedStepWorldSimulationSystemGroup</span>
      <span class="group-tag">outside the predicted chain</span>
      <span class="badge b-finalize">Port: Finalize</span>
    </div>
    <p class="purpose">Parents into Unity's
    <span class="sys">FixedStepSimulationSystemGroup</span>, <strong>outside the
    predicted chain</strong>. Its lone member,
    <span class="sys">HeroLevelUpSystem</span>, self-gates on IsFinalPredictionTick,
    rate-limits to one level per update, and directly drives managed UI
    (<span class="sys">XPGaugeUIController</span>). The group's role was authoritative,
    irreversible, UI-coupled progression that runs once per frame and never re-runs
    under rollback &mdash; the same goal as FullyPredicted, achieved by sitting outside
    the predicted loop. With rollback deprecated its distinct reason collapses;
    conceptually it folds into turn end.</p>
    <div class="members">
      <div class="member"><span class="sys">HeroLevelUpSystem</span>
        <span class="role">gem thresholds; stat multipliers</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-finalize">Finalize phase</span>
      <span class="slot">26 <i>level_up</i></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="flow">&#9660;</div>

  <!-- 9. Presentation -->
  <section class="group">
    <div class="group-head">
      <span class="group-no">9</span>
      <span class="group-name">PresentationSimulationSystemGroup</span>
      <span class="group-tag">client world, per rendered frame</span>
      <span class="badge b-client">Port: browser client</span>
    </div>
    <p class="purpose">Sprites, HUD, audio &mdash; out of simulation scope.</p>
    <div class="port"><span class="plabel b-client">Client layer</span>
      The port's browser client owns this layer entirely; the engine stays headless.
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="tick-cap">&#9650; tick N ends &mdash; tick N+1 begins at the top &#9650;</div>
</div>

<h2 id="principles"><span class="secno">3</span>Design principles &mdash; my interpretation of why the pipeline is shaped this way</h2>

<ol class="principles">
  <li><b>Single-tick completeness.</b>
    <p>The group order exists so a tick's causal chain completes inside the tick:
    intent &rarr; motion &rarr; contact &rarr; effect events &rarr; resolution &rarr;
    consequences. Producers are ordered before their consumers. The canonical
    integration mistake is placing a producer after its consumer, which silently costs
    one frame of latency (e.g. creating spawn data after the system that consumes
    it).</p></li>
  <li><b>Two-phase effects.</b>
    <p>EffectApplication determines all the consequences; EffectResolution resolves all
    the effects. The split exists precisely so that <em>ordering within a turn cannot
    change outcomes</em>: apply everything first, then convert the aggregate to state
    exactly once. Cross-source rules live at the aggregation point &mdash; hero
    max-of-same-tick-hits, i-frames starting at resolution, defeat reading post-resolve
    HP. The aggregation rule has an author-confirmed rationale: heroes become
    invulnerable for a short time when they take damage, enemies do not &mdash; so a
    hero's simultaneous same-tick hits collapse to the max (approximating one hit +
    immunity) while enemy damage sums.</p></li>
  <li><b>Structural changes at boundaries.</b>
    <p>Every predicted group ends with an ECB playback; creates and destroys are
    deferred. The spawn pipeline leans on this deliberately: wave entry &rarr; warning
    entity (ECB) &rarr; creature materializes 1.25&nbsp;s later; one warning per tick
    shapes the wave front as a trickle.</p></li>
  <li><b>Once per authoritative tick &mdash; a rollback-era artifact.</b>
    <p>Everything irreversible lives in FullyPredicted behind its gate. With
    prediction/rollback deprecated, the principle's surviving meaning is <em>turn
    end</em>: the irreversible consequences of the turn. Port equivalence: the
    Finalize phase of a fixed-step loop, where the gate is trivially true.</p></li>
  <li><b>Two clocks.</b>
    <p>The 60&nbsp;Hz tick and the 4&nbsp;Hz periodic tick; DoT cadence is a design
    constant, not a frame artifact.</p></li>
  <li><b>Integrating a new system.</b>
    <p>Choose the group by responsibility: input/flow &rarr; BeginInit; arm intents
    &rarr; WorldInit; perceive/move &rarr; WorldSim; emit effect events &rarr;
    EffectApplication; events&rarr;state &rarr; EffectResolution; irreversible
    consequences &rarr; FullyPredicted. Then order within the group: whatever consumes
    your output this tick must run after you. Times in seconds, converted by tick rate;
    config via singletons, never client-injected.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 id="flags"><span class="secno">4</span>Interpretation flags &mdash; author-reviewed</h2>

<p>Places where my reading of the source <em>corrected</em> existing docs. The author
has reviewed every claim; each entry records the original observation and the
ruling.</p>

<div class="flags">
  <div class="flag-item"><b>Pickup movement placement &mdash; resolved: confirmed wart</b>
    Observation: pickup movement lives in EffectApplication
    (<span class="sys">PickupSystem</span>), i.e. <em>after</em> MoveSystem. Author
    ruling: a wart, not intent &mdash; the code's own comments say it: "TODO: This
    system is responsible for both moving pickups and their gameplay. Those should be
    split ... so movement can happen during world simulation" and "It's a little
    strange that we're moving pickups by their own system versus just using the
    existing move system" (PickupSystem.cs). Movement is fused with consumption;
    consumption must live in EffectApplication. The flecs-ecs port already implements
    the intended split: magnet movement in the Move phase (slot 12), collection in Act
    (slot 17).</div>
  <div class="flag-item"><b>Heal-before-damage &mdash; resolved: a hack, not a rule</b>
    Observation: pickup heal commits before damage resolution, per a
    <span class="sys">PickupSystem</span> comment. Author ruling: not a design rule
    &mdash; the code declares itself a hack: "our healing system is a hack. This
    ensures the healing is applied before damage is applied, so we don't overwrite
    damage taken with our heal" (a lost-update workaround). Intent: apply all effects,
    resolve the net, clamp &mdash; order must not matter. Open edge for the author: at
    full health, take 10 + heal 10 in the same tick gives 90 in shipped Unity (heal
    clamps before damage subtracts) but 100 under the stated intent; the port
    currently yields 100.</div>
  <div class="flag-item"><b>Crowd separation &mdash; resolved: code is authoritative</b>
    Observation: crowd separation is steering anti-encroachment, not collision
    response &mdash; ally avoidance inside
    <span class="sys">CreatureMoveDirectionSystem</span> (creatures only, never
    heroes); <span class="sys">CollisionResponseSystem</span> contains no unit
    pushback despite SYSTEMS.md. Author ruling: the code is authoritative;
    documentation gets aligned to code.</div>
  <div class="flag-item"><b>DamageThisTick semantics &mdash; resolved: trust the code</b>
    Observation: <span class="sys">DamageThisTick</span> is a tick-keyed command
    buffer read by tick match, not a flag cleared each tick. Author ruling: trust the
    code.</div>
  <div class="flag-item"><b>Damage aggregation rule &mdash; resolved: confirmed intent</b>
    Observation: heroes take the MAX of same-tick hits; non-heroes the sum
    (<span class="sys">ApplyDamageSystem</span>). Author ruling: confirmed intent
    &mdash; heroes become invulnerable for a short time when they take damage, enemies
    do not, so a hero's simultaneous same-tick hits collapse to the max (approximating
    one hit + immunity) while enemy damage sums.</div>
</div>

<footer>
  Sashimi conceptual design model &mdash; interpretation from
  <code>SystemGroups.cs</code>, <code>SYSTEM-GROUPS.md</code>,
  <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> and a full source audit &mdash; 2026-07-05 &mdash;
  review against your intent &mdash; revised 2026-07-06 after author review.<br>
  Companion: <code>sandboxes/sashimi/docs/fidelity-audit.md</code>
</footer>

</main>
</body>
</html>
# Sashimi headless-AI difficulty baseline

Date: 2026-07-03 (original); re-baselined 2026-07-06 post-fidelity.

## Re-baseline (2026-07-06, post-fidelity build 455377d)

**damage_taken_ratio ~ 0.50** is the new 50% win-rate threshold
(posterior median 0.500, mean 0.507 +/- 0.013, 95% CI **[0.500,
0.550]**), from 120 games over 6 evaluations (base seed 20260703,
converged at CI width 0.05):

    ratio   wins/games  observed
    0.150      19/20      0.95
    0.250      17/20      0.85
    0.400      10/20      0.50
    0.700       0/20      0.00
    1.050       1/40      0.03

Run: `python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --min-ratio
0.1 --max-ratio 2.0 --curve 0.15,0.25,0.4,0.7 --state
build/difficulty-search/state-postfidelity.json`. The fidelity batches
(see `docs/fidelity-audit.md` "Remediation status") made the game ~12x
harder for the AI than the pre-audit build; `headless-verify` (0.5) now
sits at the balance point (its pinned seed 12345 is a winner). Caveat:
measured BEFORE Eagle FeatherRecall landed; re-verify the 0.4/0.5
points after (the search is resumable with a fresh --state file).

> The 2026-07-03 baseline below (~6.1) is a historical record of the
> pre-audit build only, kept for method reference and before/after
> comparison. Pre-audit coarse rates for context: ~99% at ratio 1.0,
> ~50% at 6.1.

## Question

The headless hero AI (`hero_ai`, slot 4) wins every tested seed at the
shipped tuning (`CombatConfig.damage_taken_ratio = 1.0`). At what ratio --
the fraction of incoming damage heroes take; the single difficulty knob of
the port -- does its win rate fall to 50%, and how high can the knob go
before the game stops being winnable half the time?

## Answer

**damage_taken_ratio ~ 6.1** (posterior median 6.09, mean 6.10 +/- 0.16,
95% credible interval **[5.80, 6.43]**), from 480 games over 24
evaluations. The headless AI is far more robust than the shipped tuning
assumes: it holds a >= 90% win rate up to ~3x damage and only falls to a
coin flip at ~6x.

## Method

Bayesian threshold search over full seeded games, adapted from the GP+EI
optimizer in `~/Dev/llm-dev/llm/lib/bayesian_concurrency_optimizer.py`
(same suggest/observe/converge architecture; model and acquisition replaced
because the observable here is a binomial win count and the target is a
50% *crossing*, not a maximum):

- **Model**: grid posterior over a floored logistic win curve
  `p_win(r; m, s, f) = f + (1 - f) / (1 + exp((r - m) / s))` with exact
  binomial likelihood per evaluation (the noise model). Uniform prior on
  the crossing location m and the floor f, log-uniform on the softness s.
- **Why a floor**: damage saturates once one hit one-shots a hero. Ratios
  8/12/16/24 produce bit-identical games per seed, and some seeds stay
  winnable there, so p_win tends to f > 0 as r grows. Without f the
  logistic fit is biased. The floor is only weakly identified by the
  near-crossing data the search concentrates on (posterior mean f ~ 0.21,
  broad); a direct scoping probe at ratios 8-24 saw 1/6 wins on a fixed
  seed panel, consistent with the posterior.
- **Reported threshold**: the derived 50% crossing
  `r50 = m + s * ln(0.5 / (0.5 - f))` -- posterior median with a 95%
  credible interval, not a point estimate.
- **Acquisition**: next ratio = argmin of the expected posterior variance
  of r50 over the binomial predictive distribution of the next 20-game
  batch (the threshold-search analogue of Expected Improvement).
- **Initial design**: fixed curve ratios 1.0 / 1.5 / 2.0 / 3.0 (also the
  reporting curve), then BO iterations until the CI width target or the
  game budget is reached. The search range starts at [1, 10] and expands
  if posterior mass piles up at the top edge.

### Run path (WASM in Node)

Each game runs the sashimi WASM module headless in Node
(`sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_runner.js`), the same fast path as
`tests/test_wasm.js`: a full 10800-tick game takes about a second. A fresh
module instance per game gives each game its own `FLECS_SEED` (via the
`ENV` runtime export in a `preRun` hook -- Emscripten does not inherit
`process.env`).

**How an arbitrary ratio enters** (root decisions 13/14/18): the WASM
`.data` bundle is baked at link time, so the on-disk `--overrides`
directory pattern cannot reach it. The runner instead writes a *generated
named config* (`difficulty-probe`) into the MEMFS copy of
`sandboxes/sashimi/app.yaml` before `wasm_init_with_config` -- the ratio
still enters via YAML named config through the exact same
`bridge_named_config` code path as the tracked `headless-verify` profile.
No engine or game C code changed; the only engine-surface change is
adding `ENV,FS` to the sashimi target's `EXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS`
(CMakeLists.txt), a test-only export surface. A native runner was
rejected because it would need a new executable target plus an on-disk
sandbox copy per probed ratio -- strictly more surface.

### Seeds policy

Deterministic and distinct per evaluation: evaluation `j` uses seeds
`base_seed + 1000*j + i` for `i in 0..N-1`, with `base_seed = 20260703`
pinned in the state file. Given a seed, a game is fully deterministic
(verified by the determinism sanity check), so every number below is
reproducible from the state file's seed lists.

### Sanity checks (`--sanity`, all PASS)

- The generated config reaches the damage math: on the same seed, the
  first hero hit lands on the same tick at ratio 1.0 and 2.0 (identical
  pre-damage trajectories) and its damage exactly doubles
  (13.125 -> 26.250).
- Cumulative hero damage rises with the ratio on the same seed.
- High-ratio (8.0) losses end as `Defeat - all heroes down` before the
  10800-tick victory time, never as timeouts.
- Same seed + same ratio replays to the identical outcome tick.

## Results

480 games, 24 evaluations of 20 games each (one evaluation ratio was
visited twice, 40 games), ~1.2 s per game through the WASM module.
Convergence stopped at the game budget with a 95% CI width of 0.64;
per-evaluation observed rates near the crossing scatter by the expected
binomial noise (SE ~ 0.11 at N=20), which the posterior integrates out.

**Threshold (50% win rate): median 6.09, mean 6.10 +/- 0.16 sd, 95% CI
[5.80, 6.43].**

Win-rate curve (observed = wins/games at that exact ratio; posterior =
model p_win with 95% band):

| ratio | wins/games | observed | posterior p_win |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.00 | 20/20 | 1.00 | 0.99 [0.98, 1.00] |
| 1.50 | 20/20 | 1.00 | 0.99 [0.96, 1.00] |
| 2.00 | 20/20 | 1.00 | 0.98 [0.95, 0.99] |
| 3.00 | 18/20 | 0.90 | 0.94 [0.89, 0.98] |
| 3.97 | 17/20 | 0.85 | 0.87 [0.79, 0.94] |
| 5.11 | 15/20 | 0.75 | 0.69 [0.61, 0.77] |
| 5.49 | 13/20 | 0.65 | 0.62 [0.55, 0.68] |
| 5.86-5.92 | 45/80 | 0.56 | 0.54 [0.48, 0.59] |
| 5.97-6.06 | 37/80 | 0.46 | 0.51 [0.46, 0.57] |
| 6.11-6.16 | 32/60 | 0.53 | 0.49 [0.44, 0.55] |
| 6.22-6.37 | 54/120 | 0.45 | 0.46 [0.39, 0.53] |

Posterior curve at round ratios: p(4.0) = 0.86, p(5.0) = 0.71,
p(6.0) = 0.52, p(6.1) = 0.50, p(6.5) = 0.43, p(7.0) = 0.37,
p(8.0) = 0.28.

Integrity of the 480 games: all 291 wins end at exactly tick 10800
(victory), all 189 losses end as `Defeat - all heroes down` strictly
before tick 10800 -- no timeouts, no ambiguous endings.

Raw per-game records (seed, outcome, final tick) live in
`build/difficulty-search/state.json`; the aggregate is in
`build/difficulty-search/summary.json`.

## How to rerun

```bash
./tools/build-wasm.sh sashimi        # build the module (once)
python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --sanity   # checks
python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --jobs 6   # search
python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --report   # re-report
```

State persists to `build/difficulty-search/state.json` after every
evaluation; the search is resumable (finished evaluations are never
re-run) and `--report` regenerates the tables without running games.
Delete the state file to start a fresh search (e.g. after AI or balance
changes -- this document describes the AI as of the commit that adds it).
# Sashimi Port Fidelity Audit

Date: 2026-07-05. Method: eight parallel read-only audits traced every
ported system to its authoritative C# under `~/Dev/sashimi/Assets/Scripts`
(file:line cited for every claim), diffed formulas, constants, trigger
conditions, per-tick semantics, and outcome-relevant ordering against
`sandboxes/sashimi/systems/native/systems.c`, plus a coverage sweep over
all 82 Unity system classes and an ordering verification of every pinned
`[UpdateBefore/After]` relation. Netcode-only behavior excluded unless it
changes effective single-player math. Docs (SYSTEMS.md, GAMEPLAY.md) were
treated as navigation only; several report-level claims below correct them.

Verdicts: EXACT (same math/semantics), EQUIVALENT (different form, provably
same outcome), APPROXIMATED (intentional simplification), DIVERGENT
(unintended behavioral difference), MISSING (no port counterpart).

## Verdict summary

| Slot | System | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| 2 | app_start | EQUIVALENT |
| 3 | game_time | APPROXIMATED (creature-level driver divergent; see #4) |
| 4 | hero_ai | EQUIVALENT (input contract; stand-in signed off) |
| 5 | start_of_tick | EQUIVALENT |
| 6 | destroy_on_timer | EQUIVALENT (timed path dead on both sides) |
| 7 | ability_machine | DIVERGENT |
| 8 | weapon_fire | DIVERGENT |
| 9 | spatial_hash | EQUIVALENT (i-frame membership detail divergent) |
| 10 | targeting | APPROXIMATED (tie-break only) |
| 11 | creature_steering | DIVERGENT |
| 12 | pickup_magnet | APPROXIMATED |
| 13 | movement | DIVERGENT (peripheral; core integration EXACT) |
| 14 | child_offset_sync | APPROXIMATED + divergent freeze gate |
| 15 | collision_push | DIVERGENT (mechanism substitution) |
| 16 | damage_apply | DIVERGENT |
| 17 | pickup_collect | EQUIVALENT |
| 18 | status_effects | DIVERGENT (poison 12x) |
| 19 | defeat | EQUIVALENT |
| 20 | iframes | APPROXIMATED (signed off; Unity value now source-pinned) |
| 21 | projectile_lifecycle | APPROXIMATED + divergent details |
| 22 | on_defeated | DIVERGENT |
| 23 | respawn | DIVERGENT |
| 24 | spawn_director | DIVERGENT |
| 25 | delayed_spawn | APPROXIMATED (timing) / DIVERGENT (HP scaling) |
| 26 | level_up | DIVERGENT |
| 27 | app_stop | EQUIVALENT |

Slots 1 (rewind) and 28 (history_capture) are engine-level, no Unity
counterpart by design.

## Top-10 remediation list (ordered by player impact)

1. **Poison deals 12x Unity damage** (status_effects). Unity per-periodic-tick
   poison = `PeriodicDamage * PeriodicTickTime / Duration` = 30 * 0.25 / 3 =
   2.5 (~30 total over 3 s) â€” `StatusEffectsSystem.cs:294`. Port deals the raw
   30 every 0.25 s (~360 total; one tick kills a slime, HP 30). The generated
   YAML comment and the generator golden check (`generate_content.py:983`)
   encode the misread. First-order hero-favor balance break.
2. **Hero pierce/bounce: replace, not add** (weapon_fire). Unity replaces the
   prefab Piercing/Bouncing with the owner's level stat and recomputes
   `HitLimit = P + B + 1` (`ProjectileLifecycleSystem.cs:167-174`;
   frog bounce = floor(level/2), eagle pierce = level,
   `LevelUpHeroSpecificSO.cs:53-72`). L1 shuriken: Unity 0 bounces / 1 hit;
   port 3 bounces / 4 hits. L20: Unity 10 bounces / 11 hits; port capped at 4.
   The difficulty baseline was tuned against the wrong weapon math.
3. **Waves spawn around the players, not at fixed arena spots**
   (spawn_director). Unity: `spawnPosition = entry.Location +
   CurrentWaveLocation`, where CurrentWaveLocation is the possessed-hero
   centroid captured at each wave advance (`EnemySpawnSystem.cs:105,139`;
   `CameraFollowPointSystem.cs:45-65` â€” "gameplay relevant, do not disable
   server simulation!"). Port anchors all 509 entries at the origin.
4. **Creature scaling: live Unity creatures never level** (game_time /
   delayed_spawn / damage_apply). The level driver is `SpawnCount`
   (creatures-spawned), whose only writer is `#if false` dead code
   (`CreatureSpawnSystem.cs:14-169,125`; `UnitInitializationSystem.cs:44-53`),
   so shipped creatures stay 1.0x damage / 1.0x HP; both multipliers are baked
   at spawn, and creature melee/ability damage is NEVER multiplier-scaled
   (only projectile spawns read DamageMultiplierStat,
   `ProjectileLifecycleSystem.cs:177-191`). Missing: creature HP x1.4 when >1
   hero alive at spawn (`UnitInitializationSystem.cs:55-58`) â€” always true for
   the port's 2-hero setup. Port instead levels creatures from shared gems
   (~1.3x dmg / ~1.84x HP late game) â€” harder AND easier than spec at once.
5. **Level-up full heal does not exist in live Unity** (level_up). The
   heal-to-full block is commented out (`HeroLevelUpSystem.cs:56-65`); real
   behavior rescales current HP by the multiplier ratio (`:143-148`). Port
   full-heals on each of ~8-11 level-ups per run. (Claim traces to a
   GAMEPLAY.md summary error.)
6. **Respawn: Unity is 10 s wait + HALF heal** (on_defeated / respawn).
   Hardcoded `UnitRespawnTime = 10.0f` (`DefeatSystem.cs:14`); respawn heals
   to `maxhealth/2` (`RespawnSystem.cs:61`). Port: 5 s + full heal. Death is
   half as punishing in the port. (Respawn i-frames 2 s: the port's guess is
   EXACT â€” `RespawnSystem.cs:17`.)
7. **Same-tick hero damage aggregation + i-frame onset/duration**
   (damage_apply / iframes). Unity sums buffers then applies MAX of same-tick
   hits to heroes (sum for non-heroes, `ApplyDamageSystem.cs:97-110`);
   i-frames start at resolution, effective NEXT tick, and last 12 ticks
   (0.2 s, `ResolveDamageSystem.cs:51,151-156`). Port applies the FIRST hit,
   starts i-frames mid-pass (blocking later same-tick attackers and skipping
   their pair/hit-budget bookkeeping), and lasts 30 ticks (0.5 s, signed off).
   Net: port heroes take less burst and have 2.5x immunity uptime.
8. **Creatures with no target idle instead of converging on the players**
   (creature_steering). Unity's no-target fallback steers creatures toward
   the possessed-hero centroid (`CreatureMoveDirectionSystem.cs:112`), so the
   whole arena presses toward the players; port zeroes velocity until a hero
   enters acquire radius 5. Arena-wide pressure/pacing change.
9. **Ability hitboxes hit one victim per swing; Unity is unlimited**
   (ability_machine + generator). All four ability prefabs serialize
   `OnHitDamageTrigger.IgnoreHitLimit = 1`; the generator extracted the
   legacy `HitLimit/ResetTime` fields, so the port caps each activation at 1
   victim. Two-hero melee: Unity hits both heroes, port hits one. Related:
   Unity enforces one active ability per caster (`AbilitySystem.cs:626-750`,
   last-ready wins) â€” the port can run cube Charge + Attack simultaneously
   with conflicting locomotion locks.
10. **Drop rolls: pity pools, not Bernoulli** (on_defeated). Unity
    accumulates `GemsPool += 0.5` / `HeartPool += 0.0375` per kill and drops
    when `Random.value < pool`, decrementing by 1 (`OnDefeatedSystem.cs:63-90`)
    â€” same means, near-zero drought risk (gem guaranteed by every 3rd kill
    early). Port rolls iid per kill; long droughts/streaks possible.

Further divergences (below top-10, still real): wave schedule +5 s shift
(wave N at N*5 s vs Unity (N-1)*5 s; port wave 36 lands exactly on the
victory tick and is never fought) and burst-materialization vs Unity's
one-warning-per-tick trickle with a 1.25 s telegraph (`spawn_delay` IS
live-consumed as the telegraph window â€” the port decision text is wrong);
collision_push is a mechanism substitution (Unity has NO unit-unit pushback
anywhere â€” separation is ally-only anti-encroachment steering inside
CreatureMoveDirectionSystem.cs:157-199, never displacing heroes or attacking
creatures; SYSTEMS.md:95 is wrong); heal-before-damage ordering inverted
(Unity commits pickup heal before damage resolution, explicit comment
`PickupSystem.cs:60-62`; port damage(16) precedes collect(17) â€” differs at
the max-HP clamp); slow multiplies the port's charge dash but Unity impulses
ignore MoveSpeedStat (`MoveSystem.cs:146-162`); ClampHeroToScreen tethers
co-op heroes to ~11x5.5 around their centroid (`MoveSystem.cs:205-228`) â€”
missing in port, no solo effect; child_offset_sync: Unity offsets X-flip
only and freeze while locomotion-disabled (aim locks at windup;
`GameplayParentAuthoring.cs:23-27`, `GameplayChildOffsetSyncSystem.cs:57-61`)
vs port full rotation + live tracking (port melee aims better than spec);
i-framed heroes leave Unity's spatial hash for the window
(`SpatialHashingSystem.cs:47,53`) but stay visible in the port; bounce fuel
= max(remaining, 50% range) not full reset (`OnHitEffectSystem.cs:131`);
failed bounce zeroes remaining bounces but keeps flying (`:173-177`) vs port
pierce-or-die; Unity bounce retarget keeps the LAST hash-order candidate (a
shipped bug, `:168-169`) vs port nearest; Unity ability/cooldown stages run
1 tick longer (`Ticks() = 1 + floor(d*60)`); ability ready checks target the
hysteretic EnemyTarget, not the nearest enemy; victory check operators give
Unity one extra gameplay tick (`> VictoryTick` vs port `>=`; heroes all dead
exactly at tick 10800 = Unity DEFEAT, port VICTORY); respawned heroes get
2 s full out-of-game in Unity (untargetable AND unable to act,
`RespawnSystem.cs:62-66`) vs port i-frames only; two hearts same tick stack
in the port (Unity last-write-wins); pickup magnet is uncapped in Unity
(baked MoveSpeed=5 is dead data) vs port cap at 5.

## Remediation status (2026-07-06)

Four implementation batches landed against this report: A `67457b9` (combat
math), B `a8bcc88` (weapons/projectiles), C `e7d0876` (waves/drops/respawn),
D `f1ec47d` (steering/movement/abilities), plus a follow-up level-up fix
(batch E closeout). Acceptance re-pinned honestly after each change: seed
12345 + `headless-verify` (damage_taken_ratio 0.5) landed on DEFEAT at tick
7591 after batch D, then reshuffled to **VICTORY at tick 10801** (gems 150,
kills 440) after the level-up proportional-rescale fix â€” that seed is one of
the ~15% winners at ratio 0.5. Final coarse win rates (20 seeds each, final
build): ratio 1.0 = 0/20 (pre-audit ~99%), 6.1 = 0/20 (pre-audit ~50%),
0.5 = 3/20, 0.25 = 19/20. Per top-10 item:

1. Poison 12x â€” **FIXED (A)**: per-pulse = 2.5, scaled by shooter damage
   mult; generator corrected, but the generated YAML comment strings stay
   stale until regeneration (extraction JSON absent on this machine).
2. Pierce/bounce replace-not-add â€” **FIXED (B)**: per-level stats replace
   the serialized base; hit budget = P + B + 1 recomputed at fire time.
3. Wave origins at players â€” **FIXED (C)**: origin = living-hero-centroid
   snapshot per wave advance. KNOWN DELTA kept: the port clamps spawn
   positions to ArenaBounds (Unity does not) â€” a pinned port decision.
4. Creature scaling â€” **FIXED (A)**: gem-driven leveling removed; 1.4x
   co-op HP at spawn added.
5. Level-up full heal â€” **FIXED (E closeout)**: caught as unremediated by
   the batch-E doc pass; current HP now rescales proportionally with the
   max multiplier (HeroLevelUpSystem.cs:143-148; the heal-to-full block at
   :56-65 is commented out in live Unity). No free heal on level-up.
6. Respawn â€” **FIXED (C)**: 10 s wait + half heal + 2 s OutOfGame.
7. Same-tick hero damage + i-frames â€” **FIXED (A)**: MAX-of-hits with full
   attacker bookkeeping (author-confirmed intent); i-frames 12 ticks,
   next-tick onset, only on resolved damage > 0.
8. No-target idle â€” **FIXED (D)**: target-less creatures converge on the
   living-hero centroid.
9. Ability hitbox limits/exclusivity â€” **FIXED (D)**: unlimited victims per
   activation; one active ability per caster (cube attack beats charge).
10. Drop rolls â€” **FIXED (C)**: pity pools (heart rolled first, both can
    drop, uniform-square unclamped placement).

Further-divergences status: wave schedule +5 s shift â€” FIXED (C), wave N
starts at (N-1)*5 s with negative carry and wave 36 is fought.
Burst-materialization â€” FIXED (C) as trace-exact, and this CORRECTS the
audit's own trickle claim above: Unity's one-warning-per-tick pacing holds
for wave 1 only; waves 2+ burst on their advance tick
(`EnemySpawnSystem.cs:86,110`), which the port now reproduces. The 1.25 s
telegraph is sim-side (C) but the CLIENT still draws it at materialization,
not the true pre-spawn window â€” DEFERRED (PendingSpawn is component-bare;
needs a dedicated export). collision_push substitution â€” FIXED (D): ally
anti-encroachment steering ported into creature_steering; collision_push
itself remains a registered no-op stub until `app.yaml` can be regenerated
(extraction JSON absent on this machine). Heal-before-damage ordering â€”
KNOWN DELTA kept per author ruling (order must not matter; the port's
arithmetic matches intent; Unity's at-full-health edge is a bug). Slow
scaling the dash â€” FIXED (D, impulses slow-immune). ClampHeroToScreen
tether â€” FIXED (D). Child-offset x-flip + windup freeze â€” FIXED (D).
I-framed units leaving the hash â€” FIXED (A). Bounce fuel
max(remaining, 50% range) â€” FIXED (B). Failed bounce forfeits bounces but
keeps flying â€” FIXED (B). Bounce retarget last-hash-order â€” KNOWN DELTA
kept: it is a shipped Unity bug whose hash iteration order is not
reproducible here; the port keeps deterministic nearest. Ability/cooldown
Ticks() = 1 + floor(d*60) â€” FIXED (D). Ability ready checks on the kept
EnemyTarget â€” FIXED (D). Victory operator strict `>` â€” FIXED (A). Respawn
OutOfGame window â€” FIXED (C). Two hearts same tick stacking â€” KNOWN DELTA
kept: the author's apply-all-then-resolve ruling makes stacking the
intent-matching arithmetic (Unity's last-write-wins is the same hack
family as the heal-ordering bug). Pickup magnet cap â€” FIXED (D, uncapped
ramp). Targeting/steering iteration order â€” KNOWN DELTA kept: snapshot
(table-creation) order stands in for Unity's irreproducible hash order,
divergence bounded by the 0.15 switch threshold.

## Coverage sweep

82 system classes found. 31 map into the 26 port systems (verified); 44 are
netcode/infra/presentation; 3 are dead code (`CreatureSpawnSystem` â€” entire
body `#if false`; `AbilityRegistrySystem` â€” body commented out;
`HelloWorldSystem`). Gameplay-relevant NO-COUNTERPART systems: 
`CameraFollowPointSystem` (wave-origin math â€” remediation #3),
`UnitInitializationSystem` (creature level + 1.4x co-op HP â€” #4),
`FollowerInputSystem` (unpossessed-hero destroy-or-follow; the port's
hero_ai stand-in is the deliberate, signed-off replacement),
`OutOfGameSystem` (full off-field semantics; port approximates with
i-frames). Known-deferred items confirmed, not counted: Eagle
FeatherRecall recall/haste loop, SlimePosse intro telegraph beats, UI
screens.

Ordering: the Unity group chain (`SystemGroups.cs:60-97`) and every pinned
intra-group relation map onto the port's phase/slot order as a valid
topological linearization, with two outcome-relevant inversions: heart heal
vs damage (above) and spawn ECB timing (Unity wave entries materialize via
end-of-group ECB and per-entry delays gate the NEXT entry â€”
`EnemySpawnSystem.cs:118-127` â€” producing the trickle; the port creates and
decrements PendingSpawns in the same Finalize pass, releasing whole waves in
one tick). The claimed weapon/ability one-tick-staleness parity is real and
verified. Note: Unity integrates projectiles inside MoveSystem
(`MoveSystem.cs:230-328`), not ProjectileLifecycleSystem â€” the port comment
at systems.c:1552-1553 misattributes; effect is a uniform one-tick shift.

## spawn_growth / spawn_delay / spawn_max (trace for the next work item)

`SpawnConfig {SpawnMax, SpawnDelay, SpawnGrowth}`
(`GameStartComponents.cs:22-27`); Adventure overrides: SpawnMax 0,
SpawnDelay 1.25, SpawnGrowth 100 (`AdventureEntities.unity:9761-9790`).
Live readers: exactly one â€” `EnemySpawnSystem.cs:143` uses **SpawnDelay** as
the warning-to-creature telegraph delay (1.25 s). **SpawnGrowth and SpawnMax
are never read by live code.** The dead continuous spawner
(`CreatureSpawnSystem.cs:85-113`, `#if false`) defines them:
`SpawnTime = SpawnDelay / 2^(seconds / SpawnGrowth)` (interval halves every
100 s), SpawnMax 0 = unlimited, and its `SpawnCount++` was the creature-level
XP source. Corrections needed: the port decision text "the global pacing
gate has no observable effect" is wrong for spawn_delay (it is the
telegraph), and `components/SpawnConfig.yaml`'s "creature level growth"
comment mislabels spawn_growth (it is spawn-rate halving for the dead
spawner). Reviving spawn_growth would mean reviving the dead spawner's
interval formula and/or SpawnCount side effect â€” a design decision, not a
fidelity fix.

## Sign-offs contradicted or pinned by source (need user re-decision)

| Signed-off value | Unity source says | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Hit i-frames 0.5 s ("not serialized") | 12 ticks = 0.2 s, hardcoded | `ResolveDamageSystem.cs:51` |
| Respawn 5 s ("not serialized") | 10.0 s, hardcoded | `DefeatSystem.cs:14` |
| Respawn full heal (doc'd as Unity behavior) | half heal | `RespawnSystem.cs:61` |
| Respawn i-frames 2 s (guess) | 2.0 s â€” guess is EXACT | `RespawnSystem.cs:17` |
| Charge dash 5 u/s ("covers max_range 2.0") | 3.0 units at 7.5 u/s | `AbilityConfigAuthoring.cs:94-97`, `AbilitySystem.cs:936-947` |
| Bounce resets full range | `max(remaining, 0.5 * range)` per bounce | `OnHitEffectSystem.cs:131` |
| Creatures level from gems ("semantics unextracted") | driver is SpawnCount; live = never levels; +1.4x HP at 2 heroes | `UnitInitializationSystem.cs:44-58`, `CreatureSpawnSystem.cs:14,125` |
| spawn_delay "no observable effect" | live telegraph window, 1.25 s | `EnemySpawnSystem.cs:143` |
| Poison ignores i-frames | poison rides the damage buffer; cleared while i-framed/OOG | `ApplyDamageSystem.cs:45-53` |

## Documentation corrections (sandboxes/sashimi/CLAUDE.md and generator)

- Slot 12 pickup_magnet: real counterpart is `PickupSystem.PickupTriggeredByJob`
  + `CollisionResponseSystem.HandlePickup` (EffectApplication, i.e. AFTER
  MoveSystem); FollowerInputSystem is the unpossessed-hero system. The
  "before Move" claim and the "capped at pickup speed" note describe the
  port, not Unity (Unity's magnet is uncapped; MoveSpeed 5 is dead data).
- Slot 3 "shared creature level from gems": cite
  `UnitInitializationSystem.cs:44-58` (SpawnCount driver, spawn-time
  application), not GAMEPLAY.md.
- Slot 9 "first in group": SpatialHashingSystem has no OrderFirst; it is
  first via consumers' `[UpdateAfter]`. Effective order identical.
- Slot 15 collision_push: Unity has no unit-unit pushback; SYSTEMS.md:95 is
  wrong. Port separation is a port-added mechanism (see remediation list).
- `MoveConfig.min_distance_to_move: 0.1` is generated but unconsumed by the
  port (Unity gates micro-movement with it, `CreatureMoveDirectionSystem.cs:145-148`).
- Wisp flee: `StartFleeDistance: 0` ships, so no creature ever flees in
  EITHER engine; the slot-11 note describes dead code.
- Unity's `DestroyOnTimerJob.SimulationTickRate` is never assigned (latent
  Unity bug: every positive timer = next-tick destroy); moot â€” no live
  producer sets a positive timer on either side.
- Port-only additions worth keeping documented: MatchStats.kills (HUD stat;
  Unity has no kill counter), hero-hero pushes in collision_push (never
  happens in Unity), deadzone 0.25 in input snap (Unity uses InputSystem
  default processors).

## Author rulings (2026-07-06 review)

The game's author reviewed the conceptual overview and ruled on the
interpretation flags; these rulings modify how the remediation list should
be executed:

- **Prediction/rollback is deprecated** (networked play abandoned). The
  FullyPredicted gate and predicted-chain determinism constraints are
  legacy; the port's one-world / every-tick-authoritative model is the
  intended end state, not a simplification. Conceptual group renames
  recorded in design-overview.html (Meta / TurnInitialization / TurnEnd).
- **Hero MAX-of-same-tick-hits: confirmed intent** â€” heroes gain brief
  invulnerability on taking damage, enemies do not, so hero same-tick hits
  collapse while enemy damage sums. Remediation #7's aggregation fix stands
  (port currently takes the FIRST hit, not the max).
- **Heal-before-damage is a self-described hack**, not a design rule
  (PickupSystem.cs: "our healing system is a hack. This ensures the healing
  is applied before damage ... so we don't overwrite damage taken with our
  heal"). Author intent: apply all effects, resolve the net, clamp â€” order
  must not matter. At-full-health edge (take 10 + heal 10 same tick):
  shipped Unity yields 90, stated intent 100, port yields 100. Ruling
  implication: keep the port's arithmetic; drop the heal-ordering item from
  the remediation list (the port matches intent, the Unity edge is a bug).
- **Pickup movement placement is an author-confirmed wart** (code TODO:
  movement should be split into world simulation). The port's split â€”
  magnet in Move (slot 12), collection in Act (slot 17) â€” implements the
  intended design; no remediation, only the CLAUDE.md attribution fix.
- **Code is authoritative over documentation** generally (crowd separation,
  DamageThisTick semantics): align docs to code, not vice versa.

## Per-system detail

The full per-system findings with both-side citations are the eight audit
transcripts merged into this report's summaries above. For anything not
covered by the remediation list or the tables, the verdict-relevant facts
are: app_start data (cell 0.5, tick rate 60, Adventure-override config
values, faction matrix bit-verified, 509 wave entries) all EXACT;
periodic clock phase/first-fire EXACT (both fire first at tick 15);
DamageThisTick equivalence holds (tick-keyed command data; no reader
observes stale entries; port TookDamage has no simulation reader);
targeting radius 5 / hysteresis 0.15 / loss rules EXACT-EQUIVALENT with
tie-break approximation bounded to 0.15 u two-hero ties; movement
integration formula EXACT incl. StatusSlow -0.75 -> x0.25 and ArenaBounds
clamp; pickup_collect trigger distance 0.025, gem accounting, heart heal
25%-of-max all EXACT/EQUIVALENT; hero level thresholds and stat
multiplier math EXACT (incl. float noise); weapon cooldowns, ability stage
durations, SCSO toggle windows, and hit-pair reset semantics EXACT or
EQUIVALENT (modulo the +1-tick Ticks() rounding); defeat predicate and
victory/defeat tie-break order EXACT.
